nodecode-channel-pidgeon is a rewrite of pidgeon-ai's pidgeon-ai/pidgeon-python:

  https://github.com/pidgeon-ai/pidgeon-python
  commit d4448663b39ae0280a247bb3ea4cab7ef0a4e080
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 PCN Labs OÜ (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the lane as a whole -- the REST client for Pidgeon's five
calls (the event log from a cursor, one message read as the Firewall's clean
text, a thread's messages, the reply, and the key's own description), the
gate that decides whether a piece of mail may become a turn (inbound only, the
answerable addresses, spam, DMARC, the Firewall's verdict), the text the agent
reads (From, To, Subject, the Firewall's warning, attachment names, then the
new body), the plain-text cleanup of an answer, and the poll that turns new
mail into a conversation and a reply into an email in the same thread. Pidgeon
serves the same API, and the endpoints are the original's.

What was left out and why: the Hermes gateway's own plumbing -- a pending
message spool on disk with a one-day replay window, the per-key platform lock,
and the gateway's session, chunking and progress-notice rules -- is the
channel kit's here, so only the cursor and the newest inbound message per
thread are kept. An answer to a card or a progress line is a reply in the
thread rather than an edit, because SMTP has no edit. Attachments are named to
the agent, never downloaded: the original read their names off the message
too. `has_more` paging past one page of events is not walked in a single lap;
the next lap reads on from the newest event the page carried.
